**Pattern:** Eulerian path (Hierholzer's algorithm)

**Key insight:** Store destinations in min-heaps; DFS greedily, and append an airport only after all its outgoing edges are used, then reverse.

```java
class Solution {
    Map<String, PriorityQueue<String>> g = new HashMap<>();
    LinkedList<String> route = new LinkedList<>();
    public List<String> findItinerary(List<List<String>> tickets) {
        for (List<String> t : tickets)
            g.computeIfAbsent(t.get(0), k -> new PriorityQueue<>()).add(t.get(1));
        visit("JFK");
        return route;
    }
    void visit(String a) {
        PriorityQueue<String> q = g.get(a);
        while (q != null && !q.isEmpty()) visit(q.poll());
        route.addFirst(a);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(E log E) / O(E)

**Pitfall:** Greedily taking the smallest neighbor without post-order insertion can strand you in a dead end.

**Pattern:** Minimum spanning tree (dense Prim's)

**Key insight:** Complete graph: O(n²) Prim with a dist array beats sorting all n² edges.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minCostConnectPoints(int[][] p) {
        int n = p.length, total = 0;
        int[] dist = new int[n];
        boolean[] in = new boolean[n];
        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        dist[0] = 0;
        for (int it = 0; it < n; it++) {
            int u = -1;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
                if (!in[i] && (u < 0 || dist[i] < dist[u])) u = i;
            in[u] = true;
            total += dist[u];
            for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) if (!in[v]) {
                int d = Math.abs(p[u][0] - p[v][0]) + Math.abs(p[u][1] - p[v][1]);
                dist[v] = Math.min(dist[v], d);
            }
        }
        return total;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n²) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Kruskal over all pairs is O(n² log n) and memory-heavy; prefer array Prim for dense graphs.

**Pattern:** Dijkstra (single-source shortest paths)

**Key insight:** Answer is the max shortest-path distance; any unreachable node means -1.

```java
class Solution {
    public int networkDelayTime(int[][] times, int n, int k) {
        List<int[]>[] g = new List[n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) g[i] = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] t : times) g[t[0]].add(new int[]{t[1], t[2]});
        int[] dist = new int[n + 1];
        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[1] - b[1]);
        pq.add(new int[]{k, 0});
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cur = pq.poll();
            if (dist[cur[0]] != Integer.MAX_VALUE) continue;
            dist[cur[0]] = cur[1];
            for (int[] e : g[cur[0]]) pq.add(new int[]{e[0], cur[1] + e[1]});
        }
        int ans = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) ans = Math.max(ans, dist[i]);
        return ans == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : ans;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(E log E) / O(N + E)

**Pitfall:** Nodes are 1-indexed; skip already-finalized nodes when popped.

**Pattern:** Minimax path (Dijkstra on max-edge)

**Key insight:** Path cost is the maximum cell on it; a min-heap keyed on that max finalizes cells in order.

```java
class Solution {
    public int swimInWater(int[][] grid) {
        int n = grid.length;
        boolean[][] seen = new boolean[n][n];
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);
        pq.add(new int[]{grid[0][0], 0, 0});
        seen[0][0] = true;
        int[] d = {0, 1, 0, -1, 0};
        while (true) {
            int[] c = pq.poll();
            if (c[1] == n - 1 && c[2] == n - 1) return c[0];
            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                int r = c[1] + d[i], col = c[2] + d[i + 1];
                if (r < 0 || col < 0 || r >= n || col >= n || seen[r][col]) continue;
                seen[r][col] = true;
                pq.add(new int[]{Math.max(c[0], grid[r][col]), r, col});
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n² log n) / O(n²)

**Pitfall:** Starting cell's own height counts — don't initialize the cost to 0.

**Pattern:** Topological sort (Kahn's BFS)

**Key insight:** Only the first differing char of each adjacent word pair gives an edge; a longer word before its own prefix is invalid.

```java
class Solution {
    public String alienOrder(String[] words) {
        Map<Character, Set<Character>> g = new HashMap<>();
        Map<Character, Integer> indeg = new HashMap<>();
        for (String w : words) for (char c : w.toCharArray()) { g.putIfAbsent(c, new HashSet<>()); indeg.putIfAbsent(c, 0); }
        for (int i = 0; i + 1 < words.length; i++) {
            String a = words[i], b = words[i + 1];
            if (a.length() > b.length() && a.startsWith(b)) return "";
            for (int j = 0; j < Math.min(a.length(), b.length()); j++) {
                char x = a.charAt(j), y = b.charAt(j);
                if (x != y) {
                    if (g.get(x).add(y)) indeg.merge(y, 1, Integer::sum);
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        Deque<Character> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : indeg.keySet()) if (indeg.get(c) == 0) q.add(c);
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            char c = q.poll();
            sb.append(c);
            for (char nx : g.get(c)) if (indeg.merge(nx, -1, Integer::sum) == 0) q.add(nx);
        }
        return sb.length() == indeg.size() ? sb.toString() : "";
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(total chars) / O(1) (alphabet-bounded)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting the prefix case ("abc" before "ab") or adding duplicate edges that inflate in-degree.

**Pattern:** Bellman-Ford limited to k+1 rounds

**Key insight:** Relax all edges k+1 times, reading from the previous round's copy so each round adds at most one edge.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findCheapestPrice(int n, int[][] flights, int src, int dst, int k) {
        int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int[] cost = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(cost, INF);
        cost[src] = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
            int[] next = cost.clone();
            for (int[] f : flights)
                if (cost[f[0]] != INF && cost[f[0]] + f[2] < next[f[1]])
                    next[f[1]] = cost[f[0]] + f[2];
            cost = next;
        }
        return cost[dst] == INF ? -1 : cost[dst];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(k · E) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Relaxing in-place lets one round chain several edges and break the stop limit.

**Pattern:** Fibonacci-style DP

**Key insight:** ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2); keep just two variables.

```java
class Solution {
    public int climbStairs(int n) {
        int a = 1, b = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) { int c = a + b; a = b; b = c; }
        return b;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Off-by-one on base cases: ways(0)=1, ways(1)=1.

**Pattern:** Linear DP

**Key insight:** best(i) = cost[i] + min(best(i-1), best(i-2)); answer is min of the last two.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minCostClimbingStairs(int[] cost) {
        int a = 0, b = 0;
        for (int i = 2; i <= cost.length; i++) {
            int c = Math.min(b + cost[i - 1], a + cost[i - 2]);
            a = b; b = c;
        }
        return b;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** The top is one past the last index — don't return the cost of landing on the last step.

**Pattern:** Linear DP (take / skip)

**Key insight:** best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + nums[i]).

```java
class Solution {
    public int rob(int[] nums) {
        int prev = 0, cur = 0;
        for (int x : nums) { int t = Math.max(cur, prev + x); prev = cur; cur = t; }
        return cur;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Greedy alternating (evens vs odds) fails, e.g. [2,1,1,2].

**Pattern:** Linear DP run twice

**Key insight:** Either skip the first house or skip the last; take the better of two linear robs.

```java
class Solution {
    public int rob(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length == 1) return nums[0];
        return Math.max(line(nums, 0, nums.length - 2), line(nums, 1, nums.length - 1));
    }
    int line(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
        int prev = 0, cur = 0;
        for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++) { int t = Math.max(cur, prev + a[i]); prev = cur; cur = t; }
        return cur;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Single-house input: both ranges become empty unless handled.

**Pattern:** Expand around center

**Key insight:** Each palindrome has a center (2n-1 of them); expand outward while ends match.

```java
class Solution {
    int start = 0, len = 0;
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) { grow(s, i, i); grow(s, i, i + 1); }
        return s.substring(start, start + len);
    }
    void grow(String s, int l, int r) {
        while (l >= 0 && r < s.length() && s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) { l--; r++; }
        if (r - l - 1 > len) { len = r - l - 1; start = l + 1; }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n²) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting even-length centers (between two chars).

**Pattern:** Expand around center

**Key insight:** Every successful expansion step is one more palindrome.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countSubstrings(String s) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int c = 0; c < 2 * s.length() - 1; c++) {
            int l = c / 2, r = l + c % 2;
            while (l >= 0 && r < s.length() && s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) { count++; l--; r++; }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n²) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Identical substrings at different positions count separately.

**Pattern:** Linear DP (1- or 2-char step)

**Key insight:** ways(i) adds ways(i-1) if s[i-1]≠'0' and ways(i-2) if the last two digits form 10..26.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numDecodings(String s) {
        int prev2 = 1, prev1 = s.charAt(0) == '0' ? 0 : 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= s.length(); i++) {
            int cur = 0;
            if (s.charAt(i - 1) != '0') cur += prev1;
            int two = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i - 2, i));
            if (two >= 10 && two <= 26) cur += prev2;
            prev2 = prev1; prev1 = cur;
        }
        return prev1;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** '0' alone is invalid and "06" is not a two-digit code.

**Pattern:** Unbounded knapsack (min)

**Key insight:** dp[a] = 1 + min over coins c of dp[a-c].

```java
class Solution {
    public int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++)
            for (int c : coins)
                if (c <= a) dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1);
        return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(amount · coins) / O(amount)

**Pitfall:** Greedy largest-coin-first is wrong for coin sets like [1,3,4], amount 6.

**Pattern:** Kadane variant tracking min and max

**Key insight:** A negative number swaps the roles of the running max and min, so keep both.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {
        int hi = nums[0], lo = nums[0], best = nums[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int x = nums[i];
            if (x < 0) { int t = hi; hi = lo; lo = t; }
            hi = Math.max(x, hi * x);
            lo = Math.min(x, lo * x);
            best = Math.max(best, hi);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Tracking only the max loses the big negative that a later negative flips positive.

**Pattern:** Prefix DP

**Key insight:** ok[i] is true if some j<i has ok[j] and s[j..i) in the dictionary.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {
        Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>(wordDict);
        boolean[] ok = new boolean[s.length() + 1];
        ok[0] = true;
        for (int i = 1; i <= s.length(); i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < i && !ok[i]; j++)
                ok[i] = ok[j] && dict.contains(s.substring(j, i));
        return ok[s.length()];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n² · L) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Plain recursion without memo is exponential on inputs like "aaaa…b".

**Pattern:** Patience sorting + binary search

**Key insight:** tails[k] = smallest tail of an increasing run of length k+1; binary-search where each number fits.

```java
class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int[] tails = new int[nums.length];
        int size = 0;
        for (int x : nums) {
            int lo = 0, hi = size;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
                if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;
            }
            tails[lo] = x;
            if (lo == size) size++;
        }
        return size;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** tails is not itself a valid subsequence — only its length is meaningful.

**Pattern:** 0/1 knapsack (subset sum)

**Key insight:** Target is sum/2; iterate sums downward so each number is used at most once.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int x : nums) sum += x;
        if (sum % 2 == 1) return false;
        int t = sum / 2;
        boolean[] can = new boolean[t + 1];
        can[0] = true;
        for (int x : nums)
            for (int s = t; s >= x; s--) can[s] |= can[s - x];
        return can[t];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n · sum) / O(sum)

**Pitfall:** Looping sums upward reuses the same item (turns it into unbounded knapsack).

**Pattern:** Grid DP (rolling row)

**Key insight:** paths(r,c) = paths(r-1,c) + paths(r,c-1); one row suffices.

```java
class Solution {
    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {
        int[] row = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(row, 1);
        for (int r = 1; r < m; r++)
            for (int c = 1; c < n; c++) row[c] += row[c - 1];
        return row[n - 1];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Combinatorics C(m+n-2, m-1) overflows int if computed with factorials.

**Pattern:** Two-string DP table

**Key insight:** Match → 1 + diagonal; else max of dropping a char from either string.

```java
class Solution {
    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String a, String b) {
        int[][] dp = new int[a.length() + 1][b.length() + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= a.length(); i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= b.length(); j++)
                dp[i][j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
                    : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
        return dp[a.length()][b.length()];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(m·n)

**Pitfall:** Subsequence ≠ substring: don't reset to 0 on a mismatch.

**Pattern:** State-machine DP

**Key insight:** Track three states per day: holding, just sold (cooldown), and resting free.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
        int hold = Integer.MIN_VALUE, sold = 0, rest = 0;
        for (int p : prices) {
            int prevSold = sold;
            sold = hold + p;
            hold = Math.max(hold, rest - p);
            rest = Math.max(rest, prevSold);
        }
        return Math.max(sold, rest);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Updating states in the wrong order lets you buy on the day right after selling.

**Pattern:** Unbounded knapsack (count)

**Key insight:** Loop coins outside, amounts inside, so each combination is counted once.

```java
class Solution {
    public int change(int amount, int[] coins) {
        int[] ways = new int[amount + 1];
        ways[0] = 1;
        for (int c : coins)
            for (int a = c; a <= amount; a++) ways[a] += ways[a - c];
        return ways[amount];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(amount · coins) / O(amount)

**Pitfall:** Swapping the loops counts permutations instead of combinations.

**Pattern:** Subset-sum counting

**Key insight:** Positives P satisfy P = (sum + target)/2, so count subsets with that sum.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findTargetSumWays(int[] nums, int target) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int x : nums) sum += x;
        if (Math.abs(target) > sum || (sum + target) % 2 != 0) return 0;
        int p = (sum + target) / 2;
        int[] ways = new int[p + 1];
        ways[0] = 1;
        for (int x : nums)
            for (int s = p; s >= x; s--) ways[s] += ways[s - x];
        return ways[p];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n · sum) / O(sum)

**Pitfall:** Zeros double the count — the downward loop with s >= 0 handles that; don't skip them.

**Pattern:** Two-string DP

**Key insight:** ok[i][j]: first i of s1 and j of s2 form the first i+j of s3; extend from the left or top cell.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isInterleave(String a, String b, String c) {
        if (a.length() + b.length() != c.length()) return false;
        boolean[] ok = new boolean[b.length() + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= a.length(); i++)
            for (int j = 0; j <= b.length(); j++) {
                if (i == 0 && j == 0) { ok[0] = true; continue; }
                char t = c.charAt(i + j - 1);
                boolean fromA = i > 0 && ok[j] && a.charAt(i - 1) == t;
                boolean fromB = j > 0 && ok[j - 1] && b.charAt(j - 1) == t;
                ok[j] = fromA || fromB;
            }
        return ok[b.length()];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Check the length sum first, or indexing c goes out of bounds.

**Pattern:** DFS + memoization on a DAG

**Key insight:** Strictly increasing edges can't cycle, so memoize the best path starting at each cell.

```java
class Solution {
    int[][] memo, m;
    int[] d = {0, 1, 0, -1, 0};
    public int longestIncreasingPath(int[][] matrix) {
        m = matrix;
        memo = new int[m.length][m[0].length];
        int best = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < m.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < m[0].length; c++) best = Math.max(best, dfs(r, c));
        return best;
    }
    int dfs(int r, int c) {
        if (memo[r][c] > 0) return memo[r][c];
        int best = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            int nr = r + d[i], nc = c + d[i + 1];
            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < m.length && nc < m[0].length && m[nr][nc] > m[r][c])
                best = Math.max(best, 1 + dfs(nr, nc));
        }
        return memo[r][c] = best;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(m·n)

**Pitfall:** No visited set needed — but without memo it's exponential.

**Pattern:** Two-string counting DP

**Key insight:** For each char of s, update counts for t positions right-to-left: if chars match, add the count of the shorter prefix.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numDistinct(String s, String t) {
        long[] dp = new long[t.length() + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++)
            for (int j = t.length(); j >= 1; j--)
                if (s.charAt(i) == t.charAt(j - 1)) dp[j] += dp[j - 1];
        return (int) dp[t.length()];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Iterating j left-to-right reuses the same s character twice.

**Pattern:** Two-string DP (Levenshtein)

**Key insight:** Match → diagonal; else 1 + min(insert, delete, replace).

```java
class Solution {
    public int minDistance(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                dp[i][j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1) ? dp[i - 1][j - 1]
                    : 1 + Math.min(dp[i - 1][j - 1], Math.min(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]));
        return dp[m][n];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(m·n)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting the base row/column (converting to/from the empty string).

**Pattern:** Interval DP (choose last to burst)

**Key insight:** Pick the balloon burst LAST in (l, r): its neighbors are then the fixed boundaries l and r.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxCoins(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length + 2;
        int[] a = new int[n];
        a[0] = a[n - 1] = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) a[i + 1] = nums[i];
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int len = 2; len < n; len++)
            for (int l = 0; l + len < n; l++) {
                int r = l + len;
                for (int k = l + 1; k < r; k++)
                    dp[l][r] = Math.max(dp[l][r], dp[l][k] + a[l] * a[k] * a[r] + dp[k][r]);
            }
        return dp[0][n - 1];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n³) / O(n²)

**Pitfall:** Thinking 'first burst' makes subproblems dependent; think 'last burst'.

**Pattern:** Two-string DP

**Key insight:** At '*': either skip the pair (j-2) or, if the preceding element matches s[i-1], consume one char and stay on the pattern.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        dp[0][0] = true;
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2] || (i > 0 && eq(s.charAt(i - 1), p.charAt(j - 2)) && dp[i - 1][j]);
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = i > 0 && eq(s.charAt(i - 1), p.charAt(j - 1)) && dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                }
            }
        return dp[m][n];
    }
    boolean eq(char c, char pc) { return pc == '.' || pc == c; }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(m·n)

**Pitfall:** Row i=0 must still be filled so patterns like "a*b*" can match the empty string.

**Pattern:** Kadane's algorithm

**Key insight:** Extend the running sum or restart at the current element — whichever is larger.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxSubArray(int[] nums) {
        int cur = nums[0], best = nums[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            cur = Math.max(nums[i], cur + nums[i]);
            best = Math.max(best, cur);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Initializing best to 0 breaks all-negative arrays.

**Pattern:** Greedy reach

**Key insight:** Track the farthest reachable index; fail if you ever stand beyond it.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canJump(int[] nums) {
        int reach = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > reach) return false;
            reach = Math.max(reach, i + nums[i]);
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Zeros are only fatal if no earlier index can jump over them.

**Pattern:** Greedy BFS by levels

**Key insight:** Each jump covers a window; the next window ends at the farthest reach from the current one.

```java
class Solution {
    public int jump(int[] nums) {
        int jumps = 0, end = 0, far = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
            far = Math.max(far, i + nums[i]);
            if (i == end) { jumps++; end = far; }
        }
        return jumps;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Looping to the last index adds an extra jump when you're already there.

**Pattern:** Greedy reset

**Key insight:** If total gas ≥ total cost a start exists; when the tank goes negative, the start must be after the current station.

```java
class Solution {
    public int canCompleteCircuit(int[] gas, int[] cost) {
        int total = 0, tank = 0, start = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < gas.length; i++) {
            int d = gas[i] - cost[i];
            total += d;
            tank += d;
            if (tank < 0) { tank = 0; start = i + 1; }
        }
        return total < 0 ? -1 : start;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Simulating from every start is O(n²) and unnecessary.

**Pattern:** Greedy from smallest (sorted counts)

**Key insight:** The smallest remaining card must start a group; consume its run.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isNStraightHand(int[] hand, int groupSize) {
        if (hand.length % groupSize != 0) return false;
        TreeMap<Integer, Integer> cnt = new TreeMap<>();
        for (int c : hand) cnt.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        while (!cnt.isEmpty()) {
            int first = cnt.firstKey();
            for (int v = first; v < first + groupSize; v++) {
                Integer k = cnt.get(v);
                if (k == null) return false;
                if (k == 1) cnt.remove(v); else cnt.put(v, k - 1);
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Starting groups from an arbitrary card instead of the minimum can fail valid hands.

**Pattern:** Greedy filter

**Key insight:** Ignore any triplet exceeding target in some slot; the rest are safe — check they cover each target value.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean mergeTriplets(int[][] triplets, int[] target) {
        boolean[] hit = new boolean[3];
        for (int[] t : triplets) {
            if (t[0] > target[0] || t[1] > target[1] || t[2] > target[2]) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) if (t[i] == target[i]) hit[i] = true;
        }
        return hit[0] && hit[1] && hit[2];
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Using a triplet with any overshooting value poisons the result forever (max never decreases).

**Pattern:** Greedy with last-occurrence

**Key insight:** Extend the current part to the last occurrence of every char seen; cut when i reaches that end.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> partitionLabels(String s) {
        int[] last = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) last[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] = i;
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        int start = 0, end = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            end = Math.max(end, last[s.charAt(i) - 'a']);
            if (i == end) { res.add(end - start + 1); start = i + 1; }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Returning boundaries/indices instead of sizes.

**Pattern:** Greedy open-count range

**Key insight:** Track min and max possible open counts; fail if max < 0, clamp min at 0, valid if min ends at 0.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkValidString(String s) {
        int lo = 0, hi = 0;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '(') { lo++; hi++; }
            else if (c == ')') { lo--; hi--; }
            else { lo--; hi++; }
            if (hi < 0) return false;
            lo = Math.max(lo, 0);
        }
        return lo == 0;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Not clamping lo at 0 lets '*' act as ')' with nothing to close.

**Pattern:** Linear sweep (before / overlap / after)

**Key insight:** Copy intervals ending before it, absorb all overlapping ones into it, then copy the rest.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] insert(int[][] intervals, int[] nw) {
        List<int[]> res = new ArrayList<>();
        int i = 0, n = intervals.length;
        while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < nw[0]) res.add(intervals[i++]);
        int lo = nw[0], hi = nw[1];
        while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= hi) {
            lo = Math.min(lo, intervals[i][0]);
            hi = Math.max(hi, intervals[i][1]);
            i++;
        }
        res.add(new int[]{lo, hi});
        while (i < n) res.add(intervals[i++]);
        return res.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Touching endpoints ([1,2] and [2,3]) count as overlapping here.

**Pattern:** Sort by start, then sweep

**Key insight:** After sorting, an interval overlaps the last merged one iff its start ≤ that end.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
        List<int[]> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] iv : intervals) {
            if (!res.isEmpty() && iv[0] <= res.get(res.size() - 1)[1])
                res.get(res.size() - 1)[1] = Math.max(res.get(res.size() - 1)[1], iv[1]);
            else res.add(new int[]{iv[0], iv[1]});
        }
        return res.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Setting the end to iv[1] instead of max(...) breaks nested intervals.

**Pattern:** Greedy by earliest end

**Key insight:** Keep the interval that ends earliest; each conflict removes one.

```java
class Solution {
    public int eraseOverlapIntervals(int[][] intervals) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));
        int removed = 0, end = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int[] iv : intervals) {
            if (iv[0] >= end) end = iv[1];
            else removed++;
        }
        return removed;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Sorting by start without care for ends keeps long intervals that block many others.

**Pattern:** Sort by start

**Key insight:** After sorting, any meeting starting before the previous one ends is a conflict.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canAttendMeetings(int[][] intervals) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++)
            if (intervals[i][0] < intervals[i - 1][1]) return false;
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** A meeting ending at 10 and another starting at 10 do not conflict.

**Pattern:** Sweep over sorted starts and ends

**Key insight:** Two sorted arrays: a start before the earliest unfinished end needs a new room.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minMeetingRooms(int[][] intervals) {
        int n = intervals.length;
        int[] s = new int[n], e = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { s[i] = intervals[i][0]; e[i] = intervals[i][1]; }
        Arrays.sort(s);
        Arrays.sort(e);
        int rooms = 0, j = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (s[i] < e[j]) rooms++;
            else j++;
        }
        return rooms;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n log n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Using <= instead of < double-books a room when one meeting ends exactly as another starts.

**Pattern:** Offline sweep + min-heap

**Key insight:** Sort intervals and queries; push intervals that started, pop those that ended, heap top is the answer.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] minInterval(int[][] intervals, int[] queries) {
        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
        Integer[] idx = new Integer[queries.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < idx.length; i++) idx[i] = i;
        Arrays.sort(idx, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(queries[a], queries[b]));
        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);
        int[] res = new int[queries.length];
        int i = 0;
        for (int qi : idx) {
            int q = queries[qi];
            while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][0] <= q) {
                pq.add(new int[]{intervals[i][1] - intervals[i][0] + 1, intervals[i][1]});
                i++;
            }
            while (!pq.isEmpty() && pq.peek()[1] < q) pq.poll();
            res[qi] = pq.isEmpty() ? -1 : pq.peek()[0];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O((n + q) log n) / O(n + q)

**Pitfall:** Answers must be written back in the original query order.

**Pattern:** Transpose + reverse rows

**Key insight:** Clockwise rotation = transpose across the main diagonal, then mirror each row.

```java
class Solution {
    public void rotate(int[][] m) {
        int n = m.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) { int t = m[i][j]; m[i][j] = m[j][i]; m[j][i] = t; }
        for (int[] row : m)
            for (int l = 0, r = n - 1; l < r; l++, r--) { int t = row[l]; row[l] = row[r]; row[r] = t; }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n²) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Transposing with j from 0 swaps every pair twice (no-op).

**Pattern:** Shrinking boundaries

**Key insight:** Walk top row, right column, bottom row, left column, then shrink; guard the last two for single rows/cols.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> spiralOrder(int[][] m) {
        List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
        int top = 0, bot = m.length - 1, left = 0, right = m[0].length - 1;
        while (top <= bot && left <= right) {
            for (int c = left; c <= right; c++) res.add(m[top][c]);
            for (int r = top + 1; r <= bot; r++) res.add(m[r][right]);
            if (top < bot) for (int c = right - 1; c >= left; c--) res.add(m[bot][c]);
            if (left < right) for (int r = bot - 1; r > top; r--) res.add(m[r][left]);
            top++; bot--; left++; right--;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(1) extra

**Pitfall:** Without the top<bot / left<right guards, a middle row or column gets added twice.

**Pattern:** Use first row/column as markers

**Key insight:** Record zero rows/cols in row 0 and col 0; track col 0's own state in a flag.

```java
class Solution {
    public void setZeroes(int[][] m) {
        int R = m.length, C = m[0].length;
        boolean col0 = false;
        for (int r = 0; r < R; r++) {
            if (m[r][0] == 0) col0 = true;
            for (int c = 1; c < C; c++)
                if (m[r][c] == 0) { m[r][0] = 0; m[0][c] = 0; }
        }
        for (int r = R - 1; r >= 0; r--) {
            for (int c = C - 1; c >= 1; c--)
                if (m[r][0] == 0 || m[0][c] == 0) m[r][c] = 0;
            if (col0) m[r][0] = 0;
        }
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Zeroing as you scan cascades zeros; apply markers bottom-up so row 0 is cleared last.

**Pattern:** Cycle detection (fast/slow)

**Key insight:** The sequence either hits 1 or loops; Floyd's tortoise-and-hare detects it in O(1) space.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isHappy(int n) {
        int slow = n, fast = next(n);
        while (fast != 1 && slow != fast) {
            slow = next(slow);
            fast = next(next(fast));
        }
        return fast == 1;
    }
    int next(int n) {
        int s = 0;
        while (n > 0) { int d = n % 10; s += d * d; n /= 10; }
        return s;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(log n) per step / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Looping until 1 without cycle detection never terminates for unhappy numbers.

**Pattern:** Carry from the right

**Key insight:** Turn trailing 9s into 0; the first non-9 just increments. All 9s → new leading 1.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] plusOne(int[] digits) {
        for (int i = digits.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (digits[i] < 9) { digits[i]++; return digits; }
            digits[i] = 0;
        }
        int[] res = new int[digits.length + 1];
        res[0] = 1;
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1) (O(n) when all 9s)

**Pitfall:** Converting to a long overflows for long digit arrays.

**Pattern:** Fast exponentiation (binary)

**Key insight:** Square the base and halve the exponent; multiply into the result on odd bits.

```java
class Solution {
    public double myPow(double x, int n) {
        long e = n;
        if (e < 0) { x = 1 / x; e = -e; }
        double res = 1;
        while (e > 0) {
            if ((e & 1) == 1) res *= x;
            x *= x;
            e >>= 1;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(log n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Negating Integer.MIN_VALUE overflows in int — widen to long first.

**Pattern:** Grade-school multiplication

**Key insight:** Digit i × digit j lands at positions i+j and i+j+1 of a result array.

```java
class Solution {
    public String multiply(String a, String b) {
        int[] pos = new int[a.length() + b.length()];
        for (int i = a.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            for (int j = b.length() - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                int sum = (a.charAt(i) - '0') * (b.charAt(j) - '0') + pos[i + j + 1];
                pos[i + j + 1] = sum % 10;
                pos[i + j] += sum / 10;
            }
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int d : pos) if (!(sb.length() == 0 && d == 0)) sb.append(d);
        return sb.length() == 0 ? "0" : sb.toString();
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(m·n) / O(m+n)

**Pitfall:** Strip leading zeros but still return "0" when a factor is zero.

**Pattern:** Hash counting over diagonal points

**Key insight:** Treat each stored point as the diagonal corner; the other two corners are determined, so multiply their counts.

```java
class DetectSquares {
    Map<Long, Integer> cnt = new HashMap<>();
    List<int[]> pts = new ArrayList<>();
    long key(int x, int y) { return ((long) x << 32) | (y & 0xffffffffL); }
    public DetectSquares() {}
    public void add(int[] p) {
        cnt.merge(key(p[0], p[1]), 1, Integer::sum);
        pts.add(p);
    }
    public int count(int[] p) {
        int res = 0;
        for (int[] d : pts) {
            if (Math.abs(d[0] - p[0]) != Math.abs(d[1] - p[1]) || d[0] == p[0]) continue;
            res += cnt.getOrDefault(key(p[0], d[1]), 0) * cnt.getOrDefault(key(d[0], p[1]), 0);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** add O(1), count O(n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Zero-area 'squares' (d equal to the query point) must be skipped; duplicates multiply.

**Pattern:** XOR cancellation

**Key insight:** x ^ x = 0 and XOR is commutative, so XOR of everything leaves the single.

```java
class Solution {
    public int singleNumber(int[] nums) {
        int x = 0;
        for (int n : nums) x ^= n;
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** A HashSet works but violates the O(1)-space follow-up.

**Pattern:** Clear lowest set bit

**Key insight:** n & (n-1) drops the lowest 1, so loop once per set bit.

```java
class Solution {
    public int hammingWeight(int n) {
        int c = 0;
        while (n != 0) { n &= n - 1; c++; }
        return c;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(set bits) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** With signed ints, use != 0 (not > 0) and >>> rather than >> if shifting.

**Pattern:** DP on bits

**Key insight:** bits[i] = bits[i >> 1] + (i & 1).

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] countBits(int n) {
        int[] res = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) res[i] = res[i >> 1] + (i & 1);
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(n)

**Pitfall:** Calling a popcount per number is O(n log n) — fine, but misses the DP point.

**Pattern:** Bit-by-bit shift

**Key insight:** Shift result left, OR in n's lowest bit, shift n right (unsigned) — 32 times.

```java
class Solution {
    public int reverseBits(int n) {
        int res = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
            res = (res << 1) | (n & 1);
            n >>>= 1;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(1) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Stopping when n hits 0 skips the trailing zeros that must become leading zeros.

**Pattern:** XOR indices with values

**Key insight:** XOR all indices 0..n with all values; pairs cancel and the missing number remains.

```java
class Solution {
    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
        int x = nums.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) x ^= i ^ nums[i];
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(n) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Forgetting to include n itself in the XOR (or in the expected sum).

**Pattern:** XOR + carry

**Key insight:** XOR adds without carry; (a & b) << 1 is the carry. Repeat until no carry.

```java
class Solution {
    public int getSum(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int carry = (a & b) << 1;
            a ^= b;
            b = carry;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(1) (≤32 iterations) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** In languages without fixed-width ints (Python) negatives loop forever without masking; Java is fine.

**Pattern:** Digit pop/push with overflow guard

**Key insight:** Before res*10 + d, check res against MAX/10 and MIN/10.

```java
class Solution {
    public int reverse(int x) {
        int res = 0;
        while (x != 0) {
            int d = x % 10;
            x /= 10;
            if (res > Integer.MAX_VALUE / 10 || res < Integer.MIN_VALUE / 10) return 0;
            res = res * 10 + d;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Time/Space:** O(log x) / O(1)

**Pitfall:** Using a long silently is disallowed by the problem's 'no 64-bit' assumption — guard explicitly.
